d directly, so I'll follow that pattern here.\n\nFor sos_120, I'll use `cast_spell` from engine.casting for tests that need the full pipeline and `on_resolve` directly for simpler cases. I'll also script player responses using `_script.appendleft` to handle choice prompts.\n\nWriting the test cases... Building the library order... I need to be careful about the deque order here — since `appendleft` adds to the left and `popleft` consumes from the left, the last item I append is the first one consumed. So I should reverse the order of my appends to match the sequence the prompts will be offered.\n\nFor testing the paradigm exile behavior, I'll set up a library with some cards, cast the capstone spell through the full pipeline to trigger the replacement event, script \"no\" responses for all the cast offers, and then verify the capstone ends up in exile. I need to use the engine's `cast_spell` rather than just calling `on_resolve` directly since that's what properly triggers the `SpellMovesToGraveyardReplacementEvent`. Let me check how `test_utils.cast_spell` works to understand what I need to pass. So the key difference is that calling `on_resolve` directly bypasses the replacement event logic, whereas going through `cast_spell` and `_resolve_top_of_stack` triggers the full resolution pipeline including the replacement manager. For the paradigm test, I need to use the complete flow to verify it actually ends up in exile, but for the simpler library exile test I can take the shortcut of calling `on_resolve` directly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":525,"cache_read_input_tokens":59605,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":525},"output_tokens":32,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d92b6c43-7322-4b8d-bfe2-1d1f7f4c24eb","uuid"